title: '`pallet-whitelist`: document that unwhitelisting pauses a live deferral rather than cancelling it, and pin it with a test'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Docs and test only. No behaviour change.

    Since #11336, `pallet-whitelist` keeps two independent storage maps: `WhitelistedCall`, owned by
    `WhitelistOrigin`, and `DeferredDispatch`, owned by `DispatchWhitelistedOrigin`. A relayed root
    dispatch needs both plus an unexpired deferral. `remove_whitelisted_call` clears only the whitelist
    entry, so a live deferral survives revocation, and re-whitelisting the same hash before expiry
    re-enables relayed execution without a fresh act from `DispatchWhitelistedOrigin`.

    This is intentional: neither origin can erase the other's record, revocation still blocks relayers
    while the hash is off the whitelist, and re-arming requires `WhitelistOrigin` again. It was
    previously undocumented and has been reported once as an authorization finding.

    Changes:
    - Pallet-level rustdoc: new "Deferred dispatch" section describing the two maps, their owners, and
      the pause-not-cancel semantics of unwhitelisting.
    - `remove_whitelisted_call` rustdoc: note that the deferred entry is left in place.
    - New test `unwhitelisting_pauses_but_does_not_cancel_deferral` pinning the behaviour.
crates:
- name: pallet-whitelist
  bump: none
